1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,256
The PR title is "register Stripe and Adyen
from plugins.yml." The reviewer leaves one
comment in red: "We are not scanning
@Component on vendor jars we do not
control.

2
00:00:11,375 --> 00:00:19,487
Metadata only." The author wanted to drop
annotated classes on the classpath and
hope component scan invents beans.

3
00:00:19,607 --> 00:00:30,335
The marketplace platform loads payment
plugins from YAML that marketing and
partnerships can edit without a rebuild of
every adapter class. That fight is about
bean definitions.

4
00:00:30,455 --> 00:00:44,543
A bean definition is the recipe Spring
holds before any instance exists: bean
name, class (or factory method), scope,
constructor arguments, property values,
lazy flag, depends-on, primary,
init/destroy method names,

5
00:00:44,663 --> 00:00:46,535
and more.

6
00:00:46,655 --> 00:01:01,415
Instances are cakes. Definitions are
recipes. You can have a recipe with zero
cakes baked yet — and you can register a
recipe for a class that lives in a jar you
must not component-scan. Look at this
example.

7
00:01:01,535 --> 00:01:09,623
# plugins.yml — marketplace payment
adapters Look at this java for a moment.
Runtime order matters.

8
00:01:09,743 --> 00:01:18,551
During context bootstrap, Spring collects
bean definitions into a
BeanDefinitionRegistry — the same
DefaultListableBeanFactory wears that hat.

9
00:01:18,671 --> 00:01:26,999
Sources of definitions include XML
readers, @Configuration class parsing,
component scanning, and programmatic
registration.

10
00:01:27,119 --> 00:01:35,255
BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor beans
run early, after the registry exists but
before most ordinary beans are
instantiated.

11
00:01:35,375 --> 00:01:46,511
In this registrar, each YAML row becomes a
GenericBeanDefinition: setBeanClassName
stores a string so the class need not be
loadable at registration time the way a
hard-coded Class<?> literal would;

12
00:01:46,631 --> 00:01:52,943
setScope marks singleton; constructor args
capture the resolved API key string from
Environment.

13
00:01:53,063 --> 00:02:00,047
registerBeanDefinition(spec.id(), def)
puts the recipe under stripeCheckout or
adyenCheckout.

14
00:02:00,167 --> 00:02:11,615
Only later, when something needs those
beans — an autowired List<PaymentPlugin>,
a lookup by name, or singleton pre-
instantiation at end of refresh — does the
factory read each definition, load the
class,

15
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:16,655
invoke the constructor with the indexed
argument, and cache the singleton.

16
00:02:16,775 --> 00:02:29,951
Change YAML, restart, new recipe set. No
@Component on Stripe's SDK required; no
vendor package added to @ComponentScan.
Walk a failure that QA hits when YAML
drifts.

17
00:02:30,071 --> 00:02:40,823
Typo in className →
CannotLoadBeanClassException or
ClassNotFoundException wrapped when the
definition is first instantiated, not when
the YAML was parsed — registration
succeeded with a bad string.

18
00:02:40,943 --> 00:02:51,023
Missing stripe.api-key →
getRequiredProperty fails inside the post-
processor and context refresh aborts
before Tomcat accepts traffic; that is the
loud failure you want.

19
00:02:51,143 --> 00:02:59,207
Duplicate id with an existing bean →
BeanDefinitionStoreException / override
behavior depending on
allowBeanDefinitionOverriding.

20
00:02:59,327 --> 00:03:09,911
Registering after ordinary singleton
instantiation has already begun is too
late for this post-processor contract —
wrong extension point, and the new names
never appear in early autowiring.

21
00:03:10,031 --> 00:03:20,687
Another symptom: partnerships edits YAML
to point at a class that exists but whose
constructor signature no longer matches
the indexed args — BeanCreationException
naming the plugin id,

22
00:03:20,807 --> 00:03:23,807
with a nested constructor mismatch.

23
00:03:23,927 --> 00:03:32,735
Trade-offs. Programmatic definitions give
marketplace teams a metadata-driven plugin
surface without scanning untrusted jars.

24
00:03:32,855 --> 00:03:39,791
You own validation: bad YAML becomes a
startup incident unless you schema-check
before registerBeanDefinition.

25
00:03:39,911 --> 00:03:49,031
Component scanning is simpler for first-
party code you control; it is the wrong
tool when the class lives in a vendor
artifact annotated for someone else's
product.

26
00:03:49,151 --> 00:03:57,407
XML <bean> elements are the same metadata
idea in a different syntax — the registry
does not care how the BeanDefinition
object was born.

27
00:03:57,527 --> 00:04:07,727
Misconception unique to bean definitions:
"The @Component class is the bean
definition." The annotation is a signal
that a scanner should create a definition.

28
00:04:07,847 --> 00:04:12,551
The definition is a separate metadata
object living in the registry.

29
00:04:12,671 --> 00:04:23,207
Two definitions can point at the same
class with different names, scopes, or
constructor args — stripeCheckout and
stripeCheckoutSandbox might share a class
and differ only in property values.

30
00:04:23,327 --> 00:04:28,391
Removing the annotation does not remove a
definition you registered by hand.

31
00:04:28,512 --> 00:04:42,983
Another misconception: "Registering a
definition creates the instance."
Registration stores the recipe; creation
happens on demand or during singleton pre-
instantiation. Partnerships ships a third
plugin next week.

32
00:04:43,103 --> 00:04:57,072
QA files a nastier bug first: two hotel-
booking browsers share one shopping cart
bean, and guest A's room selection appears
in guest B's checkout. The recipes are
fine. The scope on the cart definition is
wrong.
